WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.400
Cloud data protection starts with knowing what data exists, where it lives, who owns it,

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.580
who can access it, and how long it should be kept.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:15.280
Data may be stored in object storage, databases, file shares, queues, logs, snapshots,

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.220
backups, analytics platforms, or temporary processing locations.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.400
If the team does not know where sensitive data lives, it cannot reliably protect access,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.100
encryption, retention, or deletion.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.960
Classification gives data protection decisions a foundation. Public documentation,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.980
internal operational data, customer records, credentials, regulated data,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.480
and security logs do not all need the same handling.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.860
Classification helps teams decide which storage should be private by default,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.280
which identities may access it, which logs need protection,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.060
which backups require encryption, and which data must be deleted after a defined period.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.960
Encryption is important, but it is not a complete control by itself.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:02.740
Encryption in transit protects data moving between clients, services,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.920
and workloads. Encryption at rest protects stored data when implemented correctly.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.420
But access control and key management matter just as much. If too many identities can read

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.840
the object,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.100
query the database, or use the key, encryption will not fix the authorization problem.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.160
Keys should have owners, rotation processes, usage logging,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.260
and separation from the data they protect when possible.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.680
Object storage deserves special attention because it is widely used and easy to integrate.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:39.220
A storage bucket or container may hold application files, exports, backups, reports, logs,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.720
or customer data.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:46.140
Its access policy, public exposure setting, object-level permissions, lifecycle rules,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.360
and logging should be reviewed together.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.380
Private by default is the safer starting point. Public access should be a deliberate

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.800
design choice with clear content boundaries and monitoring.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.940
Backups are part of data security,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.720
not only availability. They should be protected from casual modification or deletion,

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.540
encrypted where appropriate, and tested through restore exercises.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.400
A backup that nobody can restore is not a recovery control. Retention should be

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.580
intentional.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.520
Keeping sensitive data forever increases risk,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.860
while deleting data too early can break recovery, compliance, or investigation needs.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.320
A practical data review asks: what data is here, who owns it,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.940
which identities can read or change it, how is it encrypted, who controls the keys,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.280
where are backups, and when should this data be deleted?

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.580
Safe cloud data protection comes from combining classification, least privilege,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.000
encryption, key management, backup testing, retention, and clear ownership.
